        "heading": "3.1 MOTIVATION",
        "text": "Starting with Neural ODEs, leveraging differential equations (DEs) has been found to be effective in modeling time-series data. They have shown promising results, however, they confront some limitations. Their model architectures and inference schemes are specialized to the DEs on which they are based, such as first-order ODE for Neural ODE. These bespoke model structures rule out their generalization ability to other classes of DEs. Consequently, they fail on many tasks close to real applications or large-scale datasets. This drawback is further exacerbated in real-world applications. Several approaches have been proposed to improve upon it (Holt et al., 2022), but they still leave much to be desired. This is confirmed by the experimental results in Figure 1 conducted on three synthetic examples proposed by (Bilo\u0161 et al., 2021). The results show that baselines fail to capture the true trajectories or produce a degraded solution.\nTherefore, we need a model that can learn a broad range of dynamics to represent a wide variety of applications. In this paper, we propose a novel framework for modeling complex systems through the lens of PDEs, a richer family of differential equations. Among recent advances in the field to learn PDE solvers, we harness FNO, which has shown promise ability to learn complex PDEs. In contrast to existing works that require the knowledge of the PDE a priori (Raissi et al., 2019; Long et al., 2019), FNO automatically learns the underlying dynamics from data. Because it can represent a wide range of dynamics in a data-driven manner, it is able to cover more diverse applications. The results depicted in Figure 1 demonstrates the ability of our model to represent more general temporal dynamics. It can be seen that our model consistently achieves superior performance in all examples. Please consult Section 4.1 for additional results and experimental details."
