        "heading": "3.4 STABILITY",
        "text": "When we learn a model for the time evolution of dynamical systems, the learned dynamics should be guaranteed to be well-posed and the stability of the model on unseen trajectories should be ensured over a long duration. This is important because the learned system can be unstable when using a generic neural network (Szegedy et al., 2013; Moosavi-Dezfooli et al., 2017). In this case, the resultant output of the learned dynamics may not be continuous with respect to its input. Also, such unstable networks are vulnerable to adversarial attacks, overfitting, and unauthorized exploitation, which may render the network useless in practice. Therefore, stability is a necessary condition in real-world applications. The stability of a model is measured by the sensitivity of the prediction with respect to small perturbations of the inputs. A formal definition is given as follows.\nDefinition 3.1. (Stability (Hadamard, 1902)) A PDE (2) is said to be stable if for any solution u (t, x) with initial condition u0 (x) and \u03f5 > 0, there exists \u03b4 > 0 such that for all new initial function u\u03030 (x) satisfying \u2225u\u03030 \u2212 u0\u2225 < \u03b4, the corresponding solution u\u0303 (t, x) satisfies \u2225u\u0303\u2212 u\u2225 < \u03f5 for all t \u2265 0.\nThe stability is a hard constraint imposed upon the model. While some recent studies have begun to address the stability of neural network architectures, it has typically been used as a soft constraint by adding an extra regularization loss term on the training data (Moosavi-Dezfooli et al., 2019), or required computation of the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix (Ross & Doshi-Velez, 2018; Hoffman et al., 2019). Because the stability of the Neural ODE corresponds to the stability of its underlying ODE, it allows the use of stable discretization techniques of the ODEs (Haber & Ruthotto, 2017; Yan et al., 2020). Similarly, the stability of CTFNO is connected to the well-posedness of the learned PDE, which is the PDE whose solution operator is the operator learned by CTFNO. The kernel formulation (3) sheds light on a way to ensure stability. A proof is deferred to appendix B.\nProposition 3.2. (Stability of CTFNO) If \u2225R (t, \u03be)\u22252 and \u2225W (t)\u22252 are bounded for every t > 0, then the corresponding CTFNO with a Lipschitz continuous activation function is stable.\nA proof is deferred to appendix B. As given in proposition 3.2, if the Fourier kernel and weight have bounded L2 norm, the global stability of CTFNO is guaranteed. Because of its expensive computational cost, however, the L2 norm of the matrix is not useful in practice. In the following, we suggest a practical method for enforcing stability conditions.\nGershgorin discs normalization Gershgorin\u2019s circle theorem (Varga, 2010) allows us to make fast deductions on the bound of eigenvalues. It states that every eigenvalue of a square matrix A lies within the union of discs centered at diagonal elements with a radius of the sum of the magnitudes of the off-diagonal entries from the same row. Therefore, as we normalize the L1 norm of each row, we can impose the requisite stability."
