at there exists Ni \u2208 N such that\n\u2225G(a)ti \u2212 GNi(a)ti\u2225Hs\u2032 < \u03f5, \u2200a \u2208 K.\nCombining all, for N > max {Ni : i = 0, . . . ,M}, we have\n\u2225G(a)t \u2212 GN (a)t\u2225Hs\u2032 \u2264 \u2225G(a)t \u2212 G(a)ti\u2225Hs\u2032 + \u2225G(a)ti \u2212 GN (a)ti\u2225Hs\u2032 + \u2225GN (a)ti \u2212 GN (a)t\u2225Hs\u2032 < 3\u03f5,\nfor any a \u2208 K and t \u2208 [0, T ]. This proves the lemma.\nThe second part is to prove that any time-dependent continuous function can be approximated by a three-layered feed forward network combined with the proposed time-module.\nLemma A.4. Let f : [0, T ]\u00d7 Rdx \u2192 Rdy be a continuous function and K \u2286 Rdx a compact subset. Then for any \u03f5 > 0, there exists a three-layered feed forward network with time\nN := Q \u25e6 L2 \u25e6 L1, L\u2113(t, x) := (t, \u03c3 (\u03c8w,\u2113(t)W\u2113x+ \u03c8b,\u2113(t)b\u2113)) , Q(t, x) = Qx,\nsatisfying \u2225N (t, x)\u2212 f(t, x)\u2225 < \u03f5, \u2200(t, x) \u2208 [0, T ]\u00d7K,\nwhere \u03c3 is an activation function, \u03c8w,\u2113 : [0, T ] \u2192 Ddv and \u03c8b,\u2113 : [0, T ] \u2192 Rdv are continuous, Q is a projection matrix, and W\u2113, b\u2113 are weight matrix and bias vector for each \u2113, respectively.\nProof. Without loss of generality, we may assume dy = 1. Construct W1 \u2208 R(dx+1)\u00d7dx and b1 \u2208 Rdx+1 as\nW1 = ( Idx 0 ) , b1 = ( 0dx 1 ) ,\nwith \u03c8w,1(t) = 0dx+1, \u03c8b,1(t) = t1dx+1. Then, L1(t, x) = ( t, (x, t)T ) \u2208 [0, T ] \u00d7 Rdx+1. By the universal approximation theorem of Cybenko (1989), for any \u03f5 > 0 there exists W2 \u2208 Rdv\u00d7(dx+1), b2 \u2208 Rdv , and Q \u2208 R1\u00d7dv such that\u2223\u2223\u2223\u2223Q\u03c3(W2 (xt ) + b2 ) \u2212 f(t, x)\n\u2223\u2223\u2223\u2223 < \u03f5, \u2200(t, x) \u2208 [0, T ]\u00d7K. By setting \u03c8w,2 = Idv , \u03c8b,2 = 1dv , we have the desired N .\nWith these lemmas out of the way, we are ready to provide a proof of Theorem 3.1. For given continuous o